  "comments": {
    "c3": {
      "anchor": "i9",
      "kind": "item",
      "label": "Lexicon names start spaces.spell, the reverse of spell.spaces, a domain that can't exist",
      "excerpt": "Lexicon names start spaces.spell, the reverse of spell.spaces, a domain that can't exist",
      "text": "Clarify",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-06T23:17:47.570Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-06T23:19:13.114Z",
          "html": "<p>In plain words:  Spell's record-type names are spelled backwards from a domain we don't own.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>\n    AT Protocol names every kind of record by a domain written backwards, the way Java names packages:\n    Bluesky's posts are <code>app.bsky.feed.post</code>, from bsky.app.\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    Spell's code (P10) names a recipe <code>spaces.spell.owen.recipes.recipe</code>:  that's\n    \"spell.spaces\" backwards.  There's no such domain:  ours is <b>spell.space</b> (Q27).\n  </li>\n  <li>It should be <code>space.spell.owen.recipes.recipe</code>:  a one-word swap in a few files, plus their tests.</li>\n  <li>\n    Why it matters:  once a name is published to the network (M11), it can't be renamed, and whoever owns the\n    domain is the one allowed to publish under it.  Nothing's published yet, so it's cheap now.\n  </li>\n  <li>The only record with the wrong name is P2's throwaway test recipe, on the test account.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Mark I9 todo and it goes into the phase being built now (P14), as its second step.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-06T23:19:11.190Z"
      }
    },
